      "title": "Flights resume across US north-east after FAA communications failure",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/21/airports-flight-ground-stop-hacking-threat",
      "description": "<p>Thousands of flights were delayed or canceled after a primary line failed and a backup fiber-optic cable was severed</p><p>Airport operations across the US north-east resumed Monday evening after a communications line failure snarled flights at some of the country’s busiest airports, leading to thousands of flight cancellations and delays.</p><p>Flights were halted at several airports after a primary communications line failed at a key Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) facility in Philadelphia. The backup fiber optic line, which delivers internet service, was inadvertently severed by a railroad construction crew, resulting in a telecommunications crash and hundreds of canceled flights at Newark, LaGuardia, JFK, Philadelphia and Boston airports.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/21/airports-flight-ground-stop-hacking-threat\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-21T23:56:30"
    },
